		"name": "Rotation Spectacle",
		"lookup_name": "Trans_RotationSpectacle",
		"description": "Rotation as procession and spectacle. Long dual corridors lead toward a rotating centerpiece, showing how orientation and angular velocity shape spatial experience over time. Gallery, down the procession: rotation makes the hole, and the hole is the way. Three plank holes - a quarter about y, a quarter about x that tips, a half turn; three where cubes roll and never pause; three slots crossed carried and turned a quarter, the other way, a full circle; two more, turned half round with the space growing, and with the space growing alone; the diagonal, the screw, the space a growing cube needs; and a last wall of three ways - a turntable you cross while it turns, a plank open at forty-five, and a bridge, which is translation made still. Mario cubes stand where the holes lead.",
